    "product_id": "dbf4656a-9c91-4d78-91fd-99d34c7ddcf7",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 191136\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n436 AM PDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* After mostly dry and low impact weather today, a deepening\n  trough will impact the region midweek with widespread rain and\n  mountain snow chances, gusty winds, and below normal \n  temperatures. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and warming\n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Weekend.\n\nToday will feature near normal temperatures with abundant\nsunshine. Other than an isolated shower or thunderstorm in the \nterrain of northern Mohave County this afternoon, it will be dry \nthrough Tuesday morning. Things change quickly starting Tuesday \nafternoon as a deepening upper level trough approaches the region.\nGusty winds will develop Tuesday afternoon in Inyo County and the\nsouthern Great Basin due to strengthen southerly flow, which will\nthen spread across the region on Wednesday. Gusts over 40 MPH and\nwind impacts will be isolated with widespread 25 to 35 MPH gusts \nexpected. Precipitation will start as early as Tuesday afternoon \nas increasing moisture and upper level diffluence set up over the \nSouthern Great Basin. Widespread precipitation chances are \npossible at times Wednesday through much of Thursday as the upper \nlevel trough moves into south- central California. With strong \nforcing and anomalous moisture in place, moderate to isolated \nheavy rain is possible and could lead to rain impacts, especially \nin southeast Nevada and western Arizona where PWATs will push \n250-300% of normal. There continues to be significant uncertainty \nin exact details as a lot will depend on how the ingredients come \ntogether- the difference between even the 25th to 75th QPF \npercentiles remains large. Snow amounts continue to fluctuate as \nwell, though increasing confidence that snow levels will remain at\nor above 9000ft and the main concern will be the high terrain of \nthe Sierra. Near normal temperatures on Tuesday, cooler than \nnormal temperatures will accompany the system as it shifts through\nthe region. \n\nThe trough should exit Thursday or Friday as it continues on to\nthe east. Precipitation chances will end west to east as it does\nso as drier air filters in behind it. By this weekend, PWATs will\nbe much lower and dry northwesterly flow should be in place- \nincreasing confidence for a dry weekend. Temperatures will remain \ncooler than normal on cool northerly flow, but should warm from \nthe temperatures associated with the peak of the rain and the \nsystem overhead.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nwinds under 8KT and following daily directional patterns will \ncontinue through the forecast period. VFR conditions will \ncontinue with a few passing clouds around 10-12kft.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow\ntypical daily directional patterns and remain around 8KT or less \nthe next 24 hours. The only exception will be KBIH in the \nnorthern Owens Valley, where southeasterly up-valley winds \ngusting to 15-20KT are expected to develop this afternoon. VFR \nconditions prevail with no operationally significant cloud cover.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
